# Skip onnxruntime-node's optional CUDA download.
#
# onnxruntime-node bundles its CPU binaries for every platform inside the npm
# package, but its postinstall (node_modules/onnxruntime-node/script/install.js)
# additionally downloads the CUDA 12 GPU execution-provider binaries from
# api.nuget.org -- and only on linux/x64, the single platform its
# install-metadata.js lists a requirement for (win32/* and darwin/* are both
# empty). That asymmetry is why this never misbehaves on a Windows or macOS dev
# machine and only ever bites on Linux: CI's `npm ci` failed with ETIMEDOUT
# reaching api.nuget.org, and WSL/Linux installs pay the same cost.
#
# The download is 182.8 MB (Microsoft.ML.OnnxRuntime.Gpu.Linux 1.29.0), of which
# the manifest extracts 3 files -- and nothing here can use them: GitHub's
# ubuntu runners have no NVIDIA card, and
# src/modules/vision/providers/personDetector.provider.js calls
# ort.InferenceSession.create() with no executionProviders, i.e. plain CPU,
# served by the bundled binaries.
#
# "skip" is onnxruntime-node's own documented opt-out; install-utils.js
# parseInstallFlag() reads this config ahead of anything else. Kept here rather
# than as an `env:` in .github/workflows/ci.yml so that Linux and WSL installs
# get it too, not just CI. Drop this line if the project ever runs on a real GPU.
onnxruntime-node-install=skip
